5^{\\circ} + \\alpha)\\)\n\nAgain, using cosine addition:\n\n\\(\\cos(45^{\\circ} + \\alpha) = \\cos45^{\\circ}\\cos\\alpha - \\sin45^{\\circ}\\sin\\alpha\\). Multiplying by -1:\n\n\\(-\\cos45^{\\circ}\\cos\\alpha + \\sin45^{\\circ}\\sin\\alpha\\)\n\nSubstituting values:\n\n\\(-\\sqrt{2}/2 \\cos \\alpha + \\sqrt{2}/2 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\nFourth term: \\(\\sin(60^{\\circ} - \\alpha)\\)\n\nUsing sine subtraction formula:\n\n\\(\\sin(60^{\\circ} - \\alpha) = \\sin60^{\\circ}\\cos\\alpha - \\cos60^{\\circ}\\sin\\alpha\\)\n\n\\(\\sin60^{\\circ} = \\sqrt{3}/2\\), \\(\\cos60^{\\circ} = 0.5\\). So substituting:\n\n\\(\\sqrt{3}/2 \\cos \\alpha - 0.5 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\nNow, let's list all expanded terms:\n\n1. \\(\\sqrt{2}/2 \\cos \\alpha - \\sqrt{2}/2 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n2. \\(-\\sqrt{3}/2 \\cos \\alpha + 0.5 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n3. \\(-\\sqrt{2}/2 \\cos \\alpha + \\sqrt{2}/2 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n4. \\(\\sqrt{3}/2 \\cos \\alpha - 0.5 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\nPlus the constants from \\(\\sin^2 30 + \\sin^2 60 = 1\\)\n\nNow, let's combine like terms. Let's group the \\(\\cos \\alpha\\) terms and the \\(\\sin \\alpha\\) terms separately.\n\nFor \\(\\cos \\alpha\\):\n\n1. \\(\\sqrt{2}/2 \\cos \\alpha\\)\n\n2. \\(-\\sqrt{3}/2 \\cos \\alpha\\)\n\n3. \\(-\\sqrt{2}/2 \\cos \\alpha\\)\n\n4. \\(\\sqrt{3}/2 \\cos \\alpha\\)\n\nAdding these together:\n\n(\\(\\sqrt{2}/2 - \\sqrt{2}/2\\)) + (\\(-\\sqrt{3}/2 + \\sqrt{3}/2\\)) = 0 + 0 = 0\n\nSo all the cosine terms cancel out.\n\nFor \\(\\sin \\alpha\\):\n\n1. \\(-\\sqrt{2}/2 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n2. \\(+0.5 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n3. \\(+\\sqrt{2}/2 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\n4. \\(-0.5 \\sin \\alpha\\)\n\nAdding these together:\n\n(-\\(\\sqrt{2}/2 + \\sqrt{2}/2\\)) + (0.5 - 0.5) = 0 + 0 = 0\n\nSo all the sine terms also cancel out.\n\nTherefore, the entire expression simplifies to the sum of the constants, which is 1.\n\nWait, that seems surprising. Let me verify again.\n\nOriginal terms:\n\n1. \\(\\sin(45 - \u03b1)\\) expanded to terms with cos \u03b1 and sin \u03b1.\n\n2. \\(-\\cos(30 + \u03b1)\\) expanded.\n\n3. \\(-\\cos(45 + \u03b1)\\) expanded.\n\n4. \\(\\sin(60 - \u03b1)\\) expanded.\n\nThen, when expanded, all variable terms (with cos \u03b1 and sin \u03b1) cancel out, leaving only the constants. The constants from \\(\\sin^2 30 + \\sin^2 60 = 0.25 + 0.75 = 1\\). So the entire expression simplifies to 1.\n\nBut let me check this with sample angles. Let's choose \u03b1 = 0\u00b0 and compute the original expression.\n\nCompute each term when \u03b1 = 0\u00b0:\n\n1. \\(\\sin(45\u00b0 - 0\u00b0) = \\sin45\u00b0 = \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.7071\\)\n\n2. \\(-\\cos(30\u00b0 + 0\u00b0) = -\\cos30\u00b0 = -\u221a3/2 \u2248 -0.8660\\)\n\n3. \\(\\sin^2 30\u00b0 = 0.25\\)\n\n4. \\(-\\cos(45\u00b0 + 0\u00b0) = -\\cos45\u00b0 = -\u221a2/2 \u2248 -0.7071\\)\n\n5. \\(\\sin(60\u00b0 - 0\u00b0) = \\sin60\u00b0 = \u221a3/2 \u2248 0.8660\\)\n\n6. \\(\\sin^2 60\u00b0 = 0.75\\)\n\nAdding all these:\n\n0.7071 - 0.8660 + 0.25 - 0.7071 + 0.8660 + 0.75\n\nLet's compute step by step:\n\nStart with 0.7071 - 0.8660 \u2248 -0.1589\n\nThen +0.25: -0.1589 + 0.25 \u2248 0.0911\n\nThen -0.7071: 0.0911 - 0.7071 \u2248 -0.616\n\nThen +0.8660: -0.616 + 0.866 \u2248 0.25\n\nThen +0.75: 0.25 + 0.75 = 1.0\n\nSo the total is indeed 1 when \u03b1 = 0\u00b0. Let's try another angle, say \u03b1 = 30\u00b0.\n\nCompute each term when \u03b1 = 30\u00b0:\n\n1. \\(\\sin(45\u00b0 - 30\u00b0) = \\sin15\u00b0 \u2248 0.2588\\)\n\n2. \\(-\\cos(30\u00b0 + 30\u00b0) = -\\cos60\u00b0 = -0.5\\)\n\n3. \\(\\sin^2 30\u00b0 = 0.25\\)\n\n4. \\(-\\cos(45\u00b0 + 30\u00b0) = -\\cos75\u00b0 \u2248 -0.2588\\)\n\n5. \\(\\sin(60\u00b0 - 30\u00b0) = \\sin30\u00b0 = 0.5\\)\n\n6. \\(\\sin^2 60\u00b0 = 0.75\\)\n\nAdding them all:\n\n0.2588 - 0.5 + 0.25 - 0.2588 + 0.5 + 0.75\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n0.2588 - 0.5 \u2248 -0.2412\n\n-0.2412 + 0.25 \u2248 0.0088\n\n0.0088 - 0.2588 \u2248 -0.25\n\n-0.25 + 0.5 \u2248 0.25\n\n0.25 + 0.75 = 1.0\n\nAgain, the result is 1. So even with different \u03b1, the expression simplifies to 1. Therefore, part b) simplifies to 1.\n\nBut let me check with \u03b1 = 45\u00b0 for thoroughness.\n\n\u03b1 = 45\u00b0:\n\n1. \\(\\sin(45\u00b0 - 45\u00b0) = \\sin0\u00b0 = 0\\)\n\n2. \\(-\\cos(30\u00b0 + 45\u00b0) = -\\cos75\u00b0 \u2248 -0.2588\\)\n\n3. \\(\\sin^2 30\u00b0 = 0.25\\)\n\n4. \\(-\\cos(45\u00b0 + 45\u00b0) = -\\cos90\u00b0 = 0\\)\n\n5. \\(\\sin(60\u00b0 - 45\u00b0) = \\sin15\u00b0 \u2248 0.2588\\)\n\n6. \\(\\sin^2 60\u00b0 = 0.75\\)\n\nAdding them:\n\n0 - 0.2588 + 0.25 + 0 + 0.2588 + 0.75\n\nCompute step by step:\n\n0 - 0.2588 = -0.2588\n\n-0.2588 + 0.25 = -0.0088\n\n-0.0088 + 0 = -0.0088\n\n-0.0088 + 0.2588 = 0.25\n\n0.25 + 0.75 = 1.0\n\nAgain, 1. So yes, regardless of \u03b1, the expression simplifies to 1. Therefore, part b) is 1.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\na) \\(\\boxed{1}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{1}\\)", "deepseek_solution": "To solve part a), we start with the expression \\(\\left(1-\\cos 15^{\\circ}\\right)\\left(1+\\sin 75^{\\circ}\\right)+\\cos 75^{\\circ} \\cos 15^{\\circ} \\cot 15^{\\circ}\\). \n\nFirst, we use trigonometric identities to simplify the terms:\n- \\(\\sin 75^{\\circ} = \\cos 15^{\\circ}\\)\n- \\(\\cos 75^{\\circ} = \\sin 15^{\\circ}\\)\n- \\(\\cot 15^{\\circ} = \\frac{\\cos 15^{\\circ}}{\\sin 15^{\\circ}}\\)\n\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(1 - \\cos 15^{\\circ}\\right)\\left(1 + \\cos 15^{\\circ}\\right) + \\sin 15^{\\circ} \\cos 15^{\\circ} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos 15^{\\circ}}{\\sin 15^{\\circ}}\n\\]\n\nExpanding the first part:\n\\[\n(1 - \\cos 15^{\\circ})(1 + \\cos 15^{\\circ}) = 1 - \\cos^2 15^{\\circ} = \\sin^2 15^{\\circ}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the second part:\n\\[\n\\sin 15^{\\circ} \\cos^2 15^{\\circ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin 15^{\\circ}} = \\cos^2 15^{\\circ}\n\\]\n\nAdding both parts together:\n\\[\n\\sin^2 15^{\\circ} + \\cos^2 15^{\\circ} = 1\n\\]\n\nFor part b), we simplify the expression \\(\\sin \\left(45^{\\circ}-\\alpha\\right)-\\cos \\left(30^{\\circ}+\\a