ing at 11 (which is 1 mod5): 11,17,23,29,35. 35 is composite. So sequence length 4.\n\nWait, but 11 mod5 is 1, so terms would be 1,2,3,4,0 mod5. So term5 is 0 mod5. Therefore, term5 is 35, which is composite. So starting at 11, you have 4 primes (11,17,23,29) before 35. So that's 4 terms.\n\nBut starting at 5, you have 5 terms (5,11,17,23,29) before 35. So that's better.\n\nSimilarly, starting at 5, the sequence is longer because the first term is 5 itself, so when you reach term 6, which is 35, that's when it becomes composite. Therefore, the maximum possible length here is 5.\n\nBut wait, maybe there's a longer sequence that starts at a higher prime and doesn't include 5. Let's check.\n\nSuppose we have a sequence that starts at a prime p >5, and suppose that none of the terms in the sequence is 5. Then, according to the earlier reasoning, one of the terms must be 0 mod5. So unless one of the terms is 5 itself, there must be a composite number in the sequence. Therefore, any arithmetic progression of primes with difference 6 must include 5 to avoid having a term divisible by 5 before the 5th term. Wait, but starting at 5 gives us 5,11,17,23,29. Then 35 is composite. So that's 5 terms. If you start at another prime, you can't get past 4 terms because you hit a multiple of 5. For example, starting at 7: 7,13,19,25. 25 is composite. So 4 terms.\n\nSimilarly, starting at 11: 11,17,23,29,35. 35 is composite. 5 terms here? Wait, no: 11,17,23,29 are primes, then 35 is composite. So that's 4 terms. Wait, wait: 11 is term1, 17 term2, 23 term3, 29 term4, 35 term5. So that's 4 primes before hitting a composite. Wait, maybe I miscalculated earlier. Let me check:\n\nStarting at 5:\n\nTerm1:5 (prime)\n\nTerm2:11 (prime)\n\nTerm3:17 (prime)\n\nTerm4:23 (prime)\n\nTerm5:29 (prime)\n\nTerm6:35 (composite)\n\nSo 5 primes. Starting at 11:\n\nTerm1:11 (prime)\n\nTerm2:17 (prime)\n\nTerm3:23 (prime)\n\nTerm4:29 (prime)\n\nTerm5:35 (composite)\n\nSo 4 primes. So the difference is because starting at 5, the first term is 5, then the next terms don't hit 5 again until term6 (35), which is composite. So starting at 5 gives longer sequence.\n\nTherefore, the longest possible arithmetic progression with difference 6 is 5 terms starting at 5: 5,11,17,23,29.\n\nBut wait, let's check other possibilities. Maybe there's a sequence that starts at a prime and skips 5 but somehow avoids hitting a multiple of 5? But according to the modulo 5 argument, that's impossible. Because if you start at a prime p \u22605, then p mod5 is 1,2,3, or4. Then, adding 6 (which is 1 mod5) each time, the sequence modulo5 cycles through p, p+1, p+2, p+3, p+4, p+5\u2261p (mod5), etc. Wait, but adding 1 each time, so after 5 steps, you cycle through all residues. Therefore, within 5 terms, one of them will be 0 mod5. Therefore, the maximum length without hitting a multiple of5 is 4 terms, but only if the starting prime is arranged such that the 5th term is the first to be 0 mod5. Wait, perhaps starting at a prime where p \u22611 mod5: then terms are 1,2,3,4,0. So the 5th term is 0 mod5. So the sequence can have 4 primes before the 5th term is composite. Similarly, starting at p\u22612 mod5: terms 2,3,4,0. So the 4th term is 0 mod5. So maximum sequence length 3 primes.\n\nWait, perhaps my earlier reasoning was slightly off. Let me clarify.\n\nIf we start at p \u22611 mod5:\n\nTerm1: p \u22611\n\nTerm2: p+6 \u22611+1=2\n\nTerm3: p+12\u22613\n\nTerm4: p+18\u22614\n\nTerm5: p+24\u22610 mod5\n\nSo term5 is 0 mod5, hence composite (unless it's 5). Therefore, sequence length 4 primes.\n\nSimilarly, starting at p\u22612 mod5:\n\nTerm1:2\n\nTerm2:3\n\nTerm3:4\n\nTerm4:0 mod5\n\nSo term4 is composite. Sequence length 3 primes.\n\np\u22613 mod5:\n\nTerm1:3\n\nTerm2:4\n\nTerm3:0 mod5\n\nSo term3 is composite. Sequence length 2 primes.\n\np\u22614 mod5:\n\nTerm1:4\n\nTerm2:0 mod5\n\nTerm2 is composite. Sequence length 1 prime.\n\nOnly if p=5, then term1:5\u22610 mod5, which is prime, then term2