lution": "To prove that the number \\( N = \\underbrace{111\\ldots1}_{1986 \\text{ ones}} \\) has at least 8 and 32 different divisors, we analyze its prime factors and apply divisor count properties.\n\n**Part a) At least 8 divisors:**\n\n1. **Prime Factors Identification:**\n   - \\( N \\) is a repunit number \\( R_{1986} \\).\n   - Repunit numbers have the property: if \\( d \\) divides \\( n \\), \\( R_d \\) divides \\( R_n \\).\n   - \\( 1986 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 331 \\), so divisors include 1, 2, 3, 6, 331, 662, 993, 1986.\n   - \\( R_2 = 11 \\) (prime), \\( R_3 = 111 = 3 \\times 37 \\) (two primes), and \\( R_{331} \\) (unknown, but at least prime or composite).\n\n2. **Distinct Prime Factors:**\n   - \\( R_{1986} \\) is divisible by \\( R_2 = 11 \\), \\( R_3 = 3 \\times 37 \\), ensuring primes 3, 11, 37.\n\n3. **Divisor Count Lower Bound:**\n   - With at least 3 distinct primes, the minimum number of divisors is \\( 2^3 = 8 \\).\n\n**Conclusion for part a:** \\( N \\) has at least 8 divisors.\n\n**Part b) At least 32 divisors:**\n\n1. **Larger Divisor Consideration:**\n   - \\( R_6 = 111111 = 3 \\times 7 \\times 11 \\times 13 \\times 37 \\) (5 distinct primes).\n   - Since 6 divides 1986, \\( R_6 \\) divides \\( R_{1986} \\).\n\n2. **Prime Factors Increase:**\n   - \\( R_{1986} \\) inherits all prime factors of \\( R_6 \\): 3, 7, 11, 13, 37.\n\n3. **Divisor Count Lower Bound:**\n   - With at least 5 distinct primes, the minimum number of divisors is \\( 2^5 = 32 \\).\n\n**Conclusion for part b:** \\( N \\) has at least 32 divisors.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\na) \\boxed{8}  \nb) \\boxed{32}", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\n\u0414\u043e\u043a\u0430\u0436\u0438\u0442\u0435, \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0447\u0438\u0441\u043b\u043e \\(11\\ldots1\\) (1986 \u0435\u0434\u0438\u043d\u0438\u0446) \u0438\u043c\u0435\u0435\u0442 \u043f\u043e \u043a\u0440\u0430\u0439\u043d\u0435\u0439 \u043c\u0435\u0440\u0435:\n\u0430) 8 \u0440\u0430\u0437\u043b\u0438\u0447\u043d\u044b\u