labeling exists for any connected graph.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that any connected graph \\( G \\) with \\( k \\) edges can be labeled with numbers \\( 1, 2, \\ldots, k \\) such that every vertex incident to two or more edges has a greatest common divisor (gcd) of 1 among its incident edge labels, we use an **ear decomposition** approach. Here's the step-by-step explanation:\n\n### **Proof**:\n\n1. **Ear Decomposition**:\n   - A connected graph \\( G \\) can be decomposed into a sequence of \"ears.\" An ear is a path where the endpoints are vertices of the existing graph, and internal vertices (if any) are new. This decomposition starts with a single edge or cycle and iteratively adds ears.\n\n2. **Labeling Strategy**:\n   - **Initialize**: Begin with an initial edge (or cycle) and label its edges consecutively starting from 1.\n   - **Add Ears Sequentially**: For each subsequent ear, label its edges with the next available consecutive integers. This ensures that at every attachment point (where the ear connects to the existing graph), the new edge label is consecutive to an existing edge label.\n\n3. **gcd Condition**:\n   - **Consecutive Labels**: Consecutive integers are coprime (gcd = 1). Thus, at vertices where an ear is attached, the existing edge label and the new edge label are consecutive, guaranteeing gcd 1.\n   - **Internal Vertices**: For internal vertices of an ear, the edges are labeled with consecutive integers, which are coprime.\n   - **High-Degree Vertices**: If a vertex has more than two edges, the gcd of all labels at that vertex is 1 because it includes at least two consecutive labels (which are coprime).\n\n4. **Example**:\n   - Consider a square graph \\( A-B-C-D-A \\):\n     - Start with edge \\( AB \\) labeled 1.\n     - Add ear \\( B-C-D-A \\): Label edges \\( BC=2 \\), \\( CD=3 \\), \\( DA=4 \\).\n     - Check gcd at each vertex:\n       - \\( A \\): gcd(1, 4) = 1.\n       - \\( B \\): gcd(1, 2) = 1.\n       - \\( C \\): gcd(2, 3) = 1.\n       - \\( D \\): gcd(3, 4) = 1.\n\n5. **Generalization**:\n   - For any connected graph, the ear decomposition ensures that all edges can be labeled such that every vertex of degree \u22652 has at least two edges with consecutive labels (gcd = 1). This satisfies the problem's condition.\n\n### **Conclusion**:\nBy systematically labeling edges using consecutive integers via an ear decomposition, we guarantee that the gcd condition is met at every vertex. Thus, such a labeling always exists for any connected graph \\( G \\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{\\text{Such a labeling is always possible.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven that $G$ is a connected graph with $k$ edges, we need to show that it is possible to label the edges of $G$ with labels $1, 2, \\ldots, k$ such that for every vertex that belongs to two or more edges, the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the labels of those edges is 1.\n\n1. **Initialization and Walk**:\n    - Start with any vertex $v_0$ of $G$. Since $G$ is connected, each vertex in $G$ has at least degree 1.\n    - Begin a walk from $v_0$ along the edges of $G$. \n    - Label the edges in the order they are encountered: label the first edge $1$, the second edge $2$, and so forth up to the $l_1$-th edge ($1 \\leq l_1 \\leq k$).\n\n    Clearly, at this point, it is possible to walk up to $k$ edges in $G$. If $l_1 = k$, then all the edges are labeled and we are done. If not, continue to step 2.\n\n2. **Continuation from Unlabeled Edges**:\n    - After labeling $l_1$ edges, at least one edge is not labeled (since $l_1 < k$).\n    - Use the connectivity of $G$ to find a new starting vertex $v_{new}$ from where there are unlabeled edges.\n    - Continue the labeling from $l_1 + 1$ for the new edges until they cannot continue further due to unlabeled edges, label these sequentially. Suppose this walk labels $l_2$ more edges ($1 \\leq l_2 \\leq k - l_1$).\n\n3. **Complete the Labeling**:\n    - Repeat the process until all edges in $G$ are labeled. Using such a strategy ensures the labels are assigned $1$ through $k$.\n\n4. **Verification of gcd Condition**:\n    - Consider any vertex $v$ that belongs to at least two edges. If $v = v_0$, one edge labeled $1$ is connected.\n    - For any other vertex $v \\neq v_0$, by construction of the labeling, whenever a new edge is labeled while revisiting $v$, the new label is a sequential integer next to the previous one. Thus, the labels are consecutive integers.\n    - The gcd of two consecutive integers, by definition, is 1, i.e., $\\gcd(a, a+1) = 1$.\n\nThus, all vertices combining at least two edges have labels whose greatest common divisor is 1.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The edges can be labeled } 1, 2, \\ldots, k \\text{ such that for each vertex the gcd of the labels of its edges is 1.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\\(\\cos 3x \\cos 6x = \\cos 4x \\cos 7x\\)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to solve the trigonometric equation cos 3x times cos 6x equals cos 4x times cos 7x. Hmm, let me start by recalling some trigonometric identities that might help here. I know that there are product-to-sum formulas which convert products of cosines into sums. Maybe that's the way to go because the equation has products of cosines on both sides. Let me write down those product-to-sum identities again to make sure I remember them correctly.\n\nThe product-to-sum formula for cosine is:\ncos A cos B = [cos(A + B) + cos(A - B)] / 2\n\nYes, that's right. So if I apply this identity to both sides of the equation, I can transform the products into sums, which might make the equation easier to solve. Let me try that.\n\nStarting with the left side: cos 3x cos 6x. Applying the formula:\n[cos(3x + 6x) + cos(3x - 6x)] / 2 = [cos(9x) + cos(-3x)] / 2\n\nSimilarly, the right side: cos 4x cos 7x becomes:\n[cos(4x + 7x) + cos(4x - 7x)] / 2 = [cos(11x) + cos(-3x)] / 2\n\nSo substi