 x=\u03c0k/10, k \u2208 \u2124.\n\nWait, but let's verify with another value. Take k=5: x=\u03c0/2. Let's plug into the original equation. Left side: cos(3*(\u03c0/2)) * cos(6*(\u03c0/2)) = cos(3\u03c0/2) * cos(3\u03c0) = 0 * (-1) = 0. Right side: cos(4*(\u03c0/2)) * cos(7*(\u03c0/2)) = cos(2\u03c0) * cos(7\u03c0/2) = 1 * 0 = 0. So both sides equal 0, so it works. Another example: k=3, x=3\u03c0/10. As checked before, that works. So seems like all x=\u03c0k/10 are solutions. But wait, let me check k=1/2, but k is integer, so no. So the solution set is x=\u03c0k/10 for all integers k.\n\nBut let me check another angle. Suppose k= -1: x= -\u03c0/10. Then cos(-3\u03c0/10)=cos(3\u03c0/10), cos(-6\u03c0/10)=cos(6\u03c0/10). So left side: cos(3\u03c0/10)*cos(6\u03c0/10). Right side: cos(-4\u03c0/10)*cos(-7\u03c0/10)=cos(4\u03c0/10)*cos(7\u03c0/10). Which is the same as before, so since cosine is even, it's valid. So negative k also work.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is x = \u03c0k/10 for any integer k. So that's the conclusion. But let me check once more.\n\nAlternatively, sometimes in trigonometric equations, we have to consider that cos A = cos B implies A = 2\u03c0k \u00b1 B. So when we had cos 9x = cos 11x, so 9x = 2\u03c0k \u00b1 11x. So solving both equations:\n\nCase 1: 9x = 2\u03c0k + 11x \u21d2 -2x = 2\u03c0k \u21d2 x = -\u03c0k = \u03c0n (since k is any integer, n = -k is also any integer).\n\nCase 2: 9x = 2\u03c0k - 11x \u21d2 20x = 2\u03c0k \u21d2 x = \u03c0k/10.\n\nTherefore, combining both cases, the solutions are x = \u03c0n and x = \u03c0k/10 for integers n and k. However, as we saw earlier, x = \u03c0n is included in x = \u03c0k/10 when k = 10n. Therefore, the complete set of solutions can be written as x = \u03c0k/10 for all integers k. Therefore, the answer is x = \u03c0k/10, k \u2208 \u2124.\n\nBut just to confirm, let me consider another approach. Suppose we subtract the right side from the left side and set to zero:\n\ncos3x cos6x - cos4x cos7x