ation of BC is 4x + 3y = 12.\n\nDistance from P(1.5, 2): |4*1.5 + 3*2 - 12| / 5 = |6 + 6 -12| /5 = |0| /5 = 0. Wait, but the midpoint of the hypotenuse in a right-angled triangle is equidistant from all vertices, but distance to BC should be zero since it's on BC. Wait, but in our coordinate system, the hypotenuse is from (3,0) to (0,4). The midpoint is indeed ((3+0)/2, (0+4)/2) = (1.5, 2), which lies on BC. Therefore, d_a = 0. The distances to the other sides:\n\nd_b is distance from P to AC (the y-axis): |1.5| = 1.5\n\nd_c is distance from P to AB (the x-axis): |2| = 2\n\nLeft-hand side: 3(0 + 1.5\u00b2 + 2\u00b2) = 3(0 + 2.25 + 4) = 3*6.25 = 18.75\n\nRight-hand side: R_a is distance from P to (0,0): sqrt(1.5\u00b2 + 2\u00b2) = sqrt(2.25 + 4) = sqrt(6.25) = 2.5\n\nR_b is distance to (3,0): sqrt( (1.5-3)^2 + (2-0)^2 ) = sqrt(2.25 + 4) = sqrt(6.25) = 2.5\n\nR_c is distance to (0,4): sqrt(1.5\u00b2 + (2-4)^2 ) = sqrt(2.25 + 4) = sqrt(6.25) = 2.5\n\nAngles: sin A = 1, sin B = 4/5, sin C = 3/5\n\nRight-hand side: (2.5*1)^2 + (2.5*(4/5))^2 + (2.5*(3/5))^2 = 6.25 + (2.5*0.8)^2 + (2.5*0.6)^2 = 6.25 + (2)^2 + (1.5)^2 = 6.25 + 4 + 2.25 = 12.5\n\nSo 18.75 \u2265 12.5, which holds.\n\nThese test cases suggest that the inequality holds, sometimes with equality in symmetric cases (like the incenter in an equilateral triangle).\n\nBut how to prove it in general?\n\nLet me return to the barycentric coordinates idea. Suppose P has barycentric coordinates (u, v, w) with u + v + w = 1. Then, the distances to the sides are proportional to u, v, w. Specifically, d_a = 2T u / a, where T is the area of the triangle, and a is the length of BC. Similarly, d_b = 2T v / b, d_c = 2T w / c.\n\nThe distances to the vertices R_a, R_b, R_c can be expressed in terms of the side lengths and the barycentric coordinates. The formula for R_a\u00b2 is:\n\nR_a\u00b2 = v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2v w b c cos \u03b1\n\nAs derived earlier.\n\nSimilarly, for R_b\u00b2 and R_c\u00b2.\n\nThen, the inequality becomes:\n\n3[( (2T u / a )\u00b2 + (2T v / b )\u00b2 + (2T w / c )\u00b2 ) ] \u2265 [ (R_a sin \u03b1 )\u00b2 + (R_b sin \u03b2 )\u00b2 + (R_c sin \u03b3 )\u00b2 ]\n\nLet\u2019s substitute R_a\u00b2, R_b\u00b2, R_c\u00b2:\n\nLeft-hand side:\n\n3*(4T\u00b2)( u\u00b2 / a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 / b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nRight-hand side:\n\n[ (v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2v w b c cos \u03b1 ) sin\u00b2 \u03b1 + (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2u w a c cos \u03b2 ) sin\u00b2 \u03b2 + (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + 2u v a b cos \u03b3 ) sin\u00b2 \u03b3 ]\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe using the Law of Sines: a / sin \u03b1 = b / sin \u03b2 = c / sin \u03b3 = 2R, where R is the circumradius.\n\nTherefore, sin \u03b1 = a / (2R), sin \u03b2 = b / (2R), sin \u03b3 = c / (2R)\n\nSo sin\u00b2 \u03b1 = a\u00b2 / (4R\u00b2), etc.\n\nTherefore, the right-hand side becomes:\n\n[ (v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2v w b c cos \u03b1 ) a\u00b2 / (4R\u00b2) + similar terms ]\n\nBut this substitution might help simplify the expression.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nTerm1 = (v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2v w b c cos \u03b1 ) a\u00b2\n\nTerm2 = (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2u w a c cos \u03b2 ) b\u00b2\n\nTerm3 = (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + 2u v a b cos \u03b3 ) c\u00b2\n\nSo right-hand side = (Term1 + Term2 + Term3 ) / (4R\u00b2)\n\nLeft-hand side = 12 T\u00b2 ( u\u00b2 / a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 / b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\nBut I need to relate T and R. Recall that T = (a b c) / (4R). So T\u00b2 = (a\u00b2 b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) / (16 R\u00b2 )\n\nTherefore, left-hand side becomes 12 * (a\u00b2 b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) / (16 R\u00b2 ) ( u\u00b2 / a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 / b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\n= (12 / 16) * (a\u00b2 b\u00b2 c\u00b2 / R\u00b2 ) ( u\u00b2 / a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 / b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 / c\u00b2 )\n\n= (3/4) * (b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 ) / R\u00b2\n\nSo, to compare the two sides:\n\nLeft-hand side: (3/4)(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 ) / R\u00b2\n\nRight-hand side: (Term1 + Term2 + Term3 ) / (4 R\u00b2 )\n\nMultiply both sides by 4 R\u00b2 to eliminate denominators:\n\nLeft-hand side: 3(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 )\n\nRight-hand side: Term1 + Term2 + Term3\n\nTherefore, the inequality reduces to:\n\n3(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 ) \u2265 Term1 + Term2 + Term3\n\nNow, compute Term1 + Term2 + Term3:\n\nTerm1 = a\u00b2 (v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2v w b c cos \u03b1 )\n\nTerm2 = b\u00b2 (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2u w a c cos \u03b2 )\n\nTerm3 = c\u00b2 (u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + 2u v a b cos \u03b3 )\n\nExpand each term:\n\nTerm1:\n\na\u00b2 v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + a\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2 a\u00b2 v w b c cos \u03b1\n\nTerm2:\n\nb\u00b2 u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2 b\u00b2 u w a c cos \u03b2\n\nTerm3:\n\nc\u00b2 u\u00b2 a\u00b2 + c\u00b2 v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + 2 c\u00b2 u v a b cos \u03b3\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n= a\u00b2 v\u00b2 b\u00b2 + a\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2 a\u00b2 v w b c cos \u03b1\n\n+ a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 + b\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2 + 2 a b\u00b2 c u w cos \u03b2\n\n+ a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + 2 a b c\u00b2 u v cos \u03b3\n\nNow, group like terms:\n\n- Terms with a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2: a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 (from Term2)\n- Terms with a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2: a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 (from Term3)\n- Terms with a\u00b2 v\u00b2 b\u00b2: a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 (from Term1)\n- Terms with b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2: b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 (from Term3)\n- Terms with a\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2: a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 (from Term1)\n- Terms with b\u00b2 w\u00b2 c\u00b2: b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 (from Term2)\n- Cross terms: 2 a\u00b2 v w b c cos \u03b1 + 2 a b\u00b2 c u w cos \u03b2 + 2 a b c\u00b2 u v cos \u03b3\n\nSo, grouping:\n\n= a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + 2 a\u00b2 v w b c cos \u03b1 + 2 a b\u00b2 c u w cos \u03b2 + 2 a b c\u00b2 u v cos \u03b3\n\nFactor common terms:\n\n= a\u00b2 b\u00b2 (u\u00b2 + v\u00b2) + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 (u\u00b2 + w\u00b2) + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 (v\u00b2 + w\u00b2) + 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 )\n\nBut note that a\u00b2 b\u00b2 (u\u00b2 + v\u00b2) + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 (u\u00b2 + w\u00b2) + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 (v\u00b2 + w\u00b2) = a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2\n\nWhich is the same as before.\n\nNow, compare this to the left-hand side: 3(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 )\n\nSo, the inequality becomes:\n\n3(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 ) \u2265 a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 )\n\nSubtract the right-hand side from both sides:\n\n3(b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 ) - [ a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 + 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 ) ] \u2265 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n= 3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 + 3a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 + 3a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 - 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 )\n\n= (3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2) + (3a\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 v\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 v\u00b2) + (3a\u00b2 b\u00b2 w\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 w\u00b2) - 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 )\n\nFactor terms in each parenthesis:\n\nFor u\u00b2 terms:\n\n= b\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2 (3) - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 u\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 u\u00b2\n\n= u\u00b2 (3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly for v\u00b2 terms:\n\n= v\u00b2 (3a\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 )\n\nFor w\u00b2 terms:\n\n= w\u00b2 (3a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 )\n\nSo, the inequality becomes:\n\nu\u00b2 (3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) + v\u00b2 (3a\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) + w\u00b2 (3a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 - b\u00b2 c\u00b2 ) - 2 a b c (a v w cos \u03b1 + b u w cos \u03b2 + c u v cos \u03b3 ) \u2265 0\n\nThis seems quite complex. However, notice that the coefficients of u\u00b2, v\u00b2, w\u00b2 can be rewritten:\n\n3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 = b\u00b2 c\u00b2 (3) - a\u00b2 (b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 )\n\nSimilarly for others.\n\nBut using the Law of Cosines, a\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 - 2b c cos \u03b1\n\nTherefore, b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + 2b c cos \u03b1\n\nSo:\n\n3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 b\u00b2 - a\u00b2 c\u00b2 = 3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a\u00b2 (a\u00b2 + 2b c cos \u03b1 )\n\n= 3b\u00b2 c\u00b2 - a^4 - 2a\u00b2 b c cos \u03b1\n\nBut this substitution seems to complicate things further.\n\nAlternatively, note that in an equilateral triangle where a = b = c, the coefficients become:\n\n3a^4 - a^4 - a^4 = a^4, and similarly for others. Then, the expression becomes:\n\nu\u00b2 a^4 + v\u00b2 a^4 + w\u00b2 a^4 - 2a^3 (a v w cos 60\u00b0 + a u w cos 60\u00b0 + a u v cos 60\u00b0 )\n\n= a^4 (u\u00b2 + v\u00b2 + w\u00b2 ) - 2a^4 (v w * 0.5 + u w * 0.5 + u v * 0.5 )\n\n= a^4 (u\u00b2 + v\u00b2 + w\u00b2 ) 