n them. Let's define the angles of points A, B, C with respect to the center.\n\nBut since point P is on the circumference, the central angles corresponding to arcs PA, PB, PC might not be straightforward. Wait, maybe parametrize the points using angles.\n\nAlternatively, let's consider coordinates. Let me place the circle with center at the origin (0,0), radius r. Let point P be at (r, 0). Then, chords PA, PB, PC make angles of 60\u00b0, 0\u00b0, and -60\u00b0 with PB? Wait, need to clarify.\n\nWait, from point P, the middle chord is PB, and the two outer chords PA and PC form 60\u00b0 angles with PB. So if PB is along a certain direction, then PA is at +60\u00b0 from PB, and PC is at -60\u00b0 from PB. But since PB is the middle chord, maybe PB is along the x-axis? Wait, but point P is already at (r, 0). If PB is along the x-axis, then point B would be diametrically opposite? Wait, no. Wait, PB is a chord from P, so if PB is along the x-axis, then point B would be another point on the circle in the direction of the x-axis from P. But since P is already at (r, 0), moving along the x-axis from P would reach a point B at (r + 2r cos \u03b8, 0), but that can't be because the circle has radius r. Wait, maybe parameterize the points using angles from the center.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using complex numbers. Let me model the circle as the unit circle in the complex plane, with point P at 1 (on the real axis). Let the three chords PA, PB, PC make angles of 60\u00b0, 0\u00b0, and -60\u00b0 with PB. Wait, but the angles at point P, which is on the circumference. Hmm.\n\nIf I consider point P as 1 in the complex plane, and chords PA, PB, PC going out from P at angles of 60\u00b0, 0\u00b0, and -60\u00b0 relative to PB. If PB is the middle chord, then PB is at some angle, say \u03b8, from the x-axis. Wait, this might get complicated. Alternatively, set PB along the x-axis. Then, from point P at (1, 0), chord PB goes to point B at some point on the circle. If PB is along the