engthwise):** Composite dimensions \\(4L \\times W\\).\n   - **2\u00d72 Layout:** Composite dimensions \\(2L \\times 2W\\).\n   - **4\u00d71 Layout (Aligned Widthwise):** Composite dimensions \\(4W \\times L\\).\n\n3. **Calculate Perimeters for Each Arrangement:**\n   - **1\u00d74 Perimeter:** \\(2(4L + W) = 8L + 2W\\).\n   - **2\u00d72 Perimeter:** \\(2(2L + 2W) = 4(L + W) = 40 \\, \\text{cm}\\) (since \\(L + W = 10\\)).\n   - **4\u00d71 Perimeter:** \\(2(4W + L) = 8W + 2L\\).\n\n4. **Relate Perimeters Using \\(L + W = 10\\):**\n   - Substitute \\(L = 10 - W\\) into perimeter expressions:\n     - **1\u00d74:** \\(8(10 - W) + 2W = 80 - 6W\\).\n     - **4\u00d71:** \\(8W + 2(10 - W) = 20 + 6W\\).\n\n5. **Solve for Given Perimeter (56 cm):**\n   - Set \\(80 - 6W = 56\\):\n     - \\(80 - 56 = 6W \\Rightarrow W = 4 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n     - Then \\(L = 10 - 4 = 6 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n   - Alternatively, set \\(20 + 6W = 56\\):\n     - \\(6W = 36 \\Rightarrow W = 6 \\, \\text{cm}\\), \\(L = 4 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n\n6. **Determine Other Perimeters:**\n   - **2\u00d72 Layout:** Always \\(40 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n   - **Remaining Perimeter:** If \\(W = 4\\), compute \\(20 + 6(4) = 44 \\, \\text{cm}\\). If \\(W = 6\\), compute \\(80 - 6(6) = 44 \\, \\text{cm}\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe perimeters of the other two rectangles are \\(\\boxed{40}\\) cm and \\(\\boxed{44}\\) cm.", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo solve the problem, we need to find the perimeters of the remaining two rectangles given that one of the three rectangles has a perimeter of 56 cm, and each individual photograph has a perimeter of 20 cm.\n\nGiven:\n- The perimeter of one rectangle: 56 cm\n- The perimeter of one photograph: 20 cm\n\nLet's analyze the problem for each case:\n\n1. **Determine the dimensions of the photographs:**\n\n   Let the dimensions of each photograph be \\( a \\) and \\( b \\).\n   \n   The perimeter \\( P \\) of a rectangle is given by:\n   \\[\n   P = 2(a + b)\n   \\]\n   For one pho