tiate \\(x f'(x)\\):\n\n\\(\\frac{d}{dx}[x f'(x)] = f'(x) + x f''(x)\\).\n\nBut the derivative of \\(n x (1 + x)^{n - 1}\\) is \\(n (1 + x)^{n - 1} + n(n - 1) x (1 + x)^{n - 2}\\).\n\nSo setting these equal:\n\n\\(f'(x) + x f''(x) = n (1 + x)^{n - 1} + n(n - 1) x (1 + x)^{n - 2}\\).\n\nBut \\(f'(x) = n(1 + x)^{n - 1}\\), so substituting:\n\n\\(n(1 + x)^{n - 1} + x f''(x) = n (1 + x)^{n - 1} + n(n - 1) x (1 + x)^{n - 2}\\).\n\nTherefore, subtracting \\(n(1 + x)^{n - 1}\\) from both sides:\n\n\\(x f''(x) = n(n - 1) x (1 + x)^{n - 2}\\).\n\nThus, \\(f''(x) = n(n - 1) (1 + x)^{n - 2}\\).\n\nBut wait, let's also compute the second derivative of \\(f(x)\\) directly. The second derivative of \\(f(x) = (1 + x)^n\\) is indeed \\(n(n - 1)(1 + x)^{n - 2}\\), so that checks out.\n\nBut how does this help with the sum involving \\(k^2\\)?\n\nLet me think. If I have the sum \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k^2\\), how can I express this in terms of derivatives?\n\nNote that:\n\n\\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k^2 = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k(k - 1) + \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k\\).\n\nWe already know the second sum is \\(n 2^{n - 1}\\). The first sum is \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k(k - 1)\\). But \\(k(k - 1)\\) is the second derivative term. Specifically, that sum is \\(n(n - 1)2^{n - 2}\\). Because if we take the second derivative of the generating function:\n\n\\(f''(x) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k(k - 1) x^{k - 2}\\), so evaluating at x=1 gives \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k(k - 1) = n(n - 1)2^{n - 2}\\).\n\nTherefore, combining the two results:\n\n\\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k^2 = n(n - 1)2^{n - 2} + n 2^{n - 1} = n(n - 1 + 2) 2^{n - 2} = n(n + 1)2^{n - 2}\\).\n\nSo that gives the sum for \\(\\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} k^2 = n(n + 1)2^{n - 2}\\). But wait, the problem here is similar but starts the sum at k=1 instead of k=0. However, when k=0, the term is 0, so the sum from k=0 is the same as from k=1. Therefore, the answer is \\(n(n + 1)2^{n -