he inequality \\(x + 3y + 14 = 0\\), substituting \\(x = -14 - 3y\\) into the quartic equation. After expanding and simplifying, we derive a quartic polynomial in \\(y\\):\n\\[\n100y^4 + 1680y^3 + 10800y^2 + 31360y + 34560 = 0.\n\\]\nDividing by 20, we get:\n\\[\n5y^4 + 84y^3 + 540y^2 + 1568y + 1728 = 0.\n\\]\nFactoring this polynomial, we find that \\(y = -4\\) is a root. Substituting \\(y = -4\\) back into the boundary equation \\(x = -14 - 3y\\), we get \\(x = -2\\).\n\nVerifying the solution \\((x, y) = (-2, -4)\\) in both the original quartic equation and the inequality confirms it satisfies both conditions. Further analysis shows that the remaining roots of the quartic equation are complex, leaving \\((x, y) = (-2, -4)\\) as the only real solution.\n\nThus, the solution to the system is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, -4)}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven system of inequalities and equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases} \nx + 3y + 14 \\leq 0, \\\\\nx^4 + 2x^2 y^2 + y^4 + 64 - 20x^2 - 20y^2 = 8xy \n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n1. First, simplify the second equation:\n   \\[\n   x^4 + 2x^2 y^2 + y^4 + 64 - 20x^2 - 20y^2 - 8xy = 0.\n   \\]\n   To make the equation more manageable, add \\(4x^2 + 4y^2\\) to both sides:\n   \\[\n   x^4 + 2x^2 y^2 + y^4 + 64 - 20x^2 - 20y^2 + 4x^2 + 4y^2 = 8xy + 4x^2 + 4y^2,\n   \\]\n   which simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   (x^2 + y^2)^2 + 64 - 16x^2 - 16y^2 = 4x^2 + 4y^2 + 8xy.\n   \\]\n   This can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   \\left(x^2 + y^2 - 8\\right)^2 = (2x + 2y)^2.\n   \\]\n   \n2. Note that the above equation implies a system of equations which can branch into:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases} \n   x^2 + y^2 - 8 = 2x + 2y, \\\\\n   x^2 + y^2 - 8 = -2x - 2y \n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n3. For the first branch:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 - 8 = 2x + 2y,\n   \\]\n   rearrange it into standard form:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 2x + y^2 - 2y = 10,\n   \\]\n   which can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 = 10.\n   \\]\n   This represents a circle centered at \\((1, 1)\\) with radius \\(\\sqrt{10}\\).\n\n4. For the second branch:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 - 8 = -2x - 2y,\n   \\]\n   rearrange it into standard form:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 2x + y^2 + 2y = 10,\n   \\]\n   which can be rewritten as:\n   \\[\n   (x+1)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 10.\n   \\]\n   This represents a circle centered at \\((-1, -1)\\) with radius \\(\\sqrt{10}\\).\n\n5. Analyze the inequality \\( x + 3y + 14 \\leq 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x \\leq -3y - 14.\n   \\]\n   Consider the line \\( x = -3y - 14 \\) and determine where it intersects the two circles:\n   \n   a) For the circle \\((x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 = 10\\):\n      \\[\n      \\begin{cases} \n      (x-1)^2 + (y-1)^2 = 10, \\\\\n      x = -3y - 14 \n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      Substitute \\( x = -3y - 14 \\) into the circle equation:\n      \\[\n      (-3y - 15)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 10,\n      \\]\n      simplify:\n      \\[\n      10y^2 + 88y + 226 = 0.\n      \\]\n      Solving this quadratic equation shows no real solution, indicating no intersection.\n\n   b) For the circle \\((x+1)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 10\\):\n      \\[\n      \\begin{cases} \n      (x+1)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 10, \\\\\n      x = -3y - 14 \n      \\end{cases}\n      \\]\n      Substitute \\( x = -3y - 14 \\) into the circle equation:\n      \\[\n      (-3y - 13)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 10,\n      \\]\n      simplify:\n      \\[\n      10y^2 + 80y + 160 = 0.\n      \\]\n      Solving this quadratic equation yields \\( y = -4 \\). Substitute back, \\( x = -3(-4) - 14 = -2 \\).\n      \n6. Verify whether \\((-2, -4)\\) lies in the semi-plane defined by \\( x + 3y + 14 \\leq 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   -2 + 3(-4) + 14 = -2 - 12 + 14 = 0.\n   \\]\n   This satisfies the inequality.\n   \n### Conclusion:\nThe solution to the system is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(-2, -4)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There are two ingots of different alloys of copper and tin weighing 6 kg and 12 kg, respectively. Identical pieces were cut from eac