ith the remnants of the first ingot. The key is that the ratio of copper to tin in both new ingots is the same.\n\nLet \\( c_1 \\) and \\( c_2 \\) be the fractions of copper in the first and second ingots, respectively. The fractions of tin will then be \\( 1 - c_1 \\) and \\( 1 - c_2 \\).\n\nFor the first new ingot (formed by fusing \\( x \\) kg from the first ingot with \\( 12 - x \\) kg from the second ingot):\n- Copper: \\( x c_1 + (12 - x) c_2 \\)\n- Tin: \\( x (1 - c_1) + (12 - x) (1 - c_2) \\)\n\nFor the second new ingot (formed by fusing \\( x \\) kg from the second ingot with \\( 6 - x \\) kg from the first ingot):\n- Copper: \\( x c_2 + (6 - x) c_1 \\)\n- Tin: \\( x (1 - c_2) + (6 - x) (1 - c_1) \\)\n\nSetting the ratios of copper to tin equal for both new ingots, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{x c_1 + (12 - x) c_2}{x (1 - c_1) + (12 - x) (1 - c_2)} = \\frac{x c_2 + (6 - x) c_1}{x (1 - c_2) + (6 - x) (1 - c_1)}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying and simplifying both sides, we find that the equation must hold for all \\( c_1 \\) and \\( c_2 \\). After expanding and simplifying, we find that the terms involving \\( c_1 \\) and \\( c_2 \\) factor out, leading to:\n\\[\n18x (c_1 - c_2) - 72 (c_1 - c_2) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the ingots are different alloys, \\( c_1 \\neq c_2 \\), so we solve:\n\\[\n18x - 72 = 0 \\implies x = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the weight of each cut piece is \\(\\boxed{4}\\) kilograms.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let the weights of each of the cut pieces be denoted by \\( x \\) kilograms.\n2. Define the fraction of tin in the first and second ingots as \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), respectively, where \\( a \\neq b \\).\n   \n3. The weight of the first ingot is 6 kg, and the weight of the second ingot is 12 kg.\n   \n4. After cutting, we merge the first piece (weight \\( x \\)) with the remaining part of the second ingot, and merge the second piece (weight \\( x \\)) with the remaining part of the first ingot.\n\n5. The remaining part of the first ingot weighs \\( 6 - x