rd. By rationalizing the numerator using the difference of squares, we can avoid expanding the square roots with Taylor series. This seems cleaner.\n\nSo, to recap:\n\n1. Recognize the numerator as a difference of square roots.\n2. Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate to rationalize.\n3. Simplify the resulting expression.\n4. Divide by sqrt(n) and simplify each term.\n5. Take the limit as n approaches infinity.\n\nThis method leads us directly to the result of -5/2 without needing to go through Taylor series expansions, which is more elegant and less error-prone.\n\nTherefore, the limit is indeed -5/2.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{-\\dfrac{5}{2}}", "deepseek_solution": "To find the limit of the sequence:\n\n$$\\lim _{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\sqrt{n^{5}-8}-n \\sqrt{n\\left(n^{2}+5\\right)}}{\\sqrt{n}}$$\n\nwe start by simplifying the numerator. \n\n1. **Simplify the terms in the numerator:**\n   - For the first term, \\(\\sqrt{n^5 - 8}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{n^5 - 8} = \\sqrt{n^5 \\left(1 - \\frac{8}{n^5}\\right)} = n^{5/2} \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{8}{n^5}}\n     \\]\n   - For the second term, \\(n \\sqrt{n(n^2 + 5)}\\):\n     \\[\n     n \\sqrt{n(n^2 + 5)} = n \\sqrt{n^3 + 5n} = n \\sqrt{n^3 \\left(1 + \\frac{5}{n^2}\\right)} = n^{5/2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{5}{n^2}}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Combine the terms in the numerator:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{n^5 - 8} - n \\sqrt{n(n^2 + 5)} = n^{5/2} \\left[ \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{8}{n^5}} - \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{5}{n^2}} \\right]\n   \\]\n\n3. **Divide by \\(\\sqrt{n}\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^{5/2} \\left[ \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{8}{n^5}} - \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{5}{n^2}} \\right]}{\\sqrt{n}} = n^2 \\left[ \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{8}{n^5}} - \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{5}{n^2}} \\right]\n   \\]\n\n4. **Rationalize the difference of square roots:**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{n^5 - 8} - \\sqrt{n^5 + 5n^3} = \\frac{(n^5 - 8) - (n^5 + 5n^3)}{\\sqrt{n^5 - 8} + \\sqrt{n^5 + 5n^3}} = \\frac{-5n^3 - 8}{\\sqrt{n^5 - 8} + \\sqrt{n^5 + 5n^3}}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the 