omes 2*(-1/2)*cos2A = -cos2A\n\nTherefore, the total cosine terms become cos2A + (-cos2A) = 0\n\nTherefore, the entire expression simplifies to 3/2 + 0 /2 = 3/2.\n\nWait, so the total is 3/2. That seems straightforward. Let me check with a specific angle. Let's take A=0\u00b0.\n\nCompute cos\u00b20 + cos\u00b260\u00b0 + cos\u00b260\u00b0 = 1 + (0.5)^2 + (0.5)^2 = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 = 1.5 = 3/2. Correct.\n\nAnother angle, say A=30\u00b0.\n\ncos\u00b230\u00b0 + cos\u00b230\u00b0 + cos\u00b290\u00b0 = (\u221a3/2)^2 + (\u221a3/2)^2 + 0 = 3/4 + 3/4 + 0 = 3/2. Correct.\n\nSo the identity holds. Therefore, the value is 3/2 regardless of A.\n\nThus, the answer is 3/2.\n\nNow problem (4): cos \u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 2\u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 3\u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 4\u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 5\u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 6\u03c0/15 \u00b7 cos 7\u03c0/15.\n\nFirst, note that \u03c0/15 is 12\u00b0, so the angles are 12\u00b0, 24\u00b0, 36\u00b0, 48\u00b0, 60\u00b0, 72\u00b0, 84\u00b0. Let's see if we can simplify this product.\n\nFirst, note that 3\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/5 = 36\u00b0, 5\u03c0/15 = \u03c0/3 = 60\u00b0, 6\u03c0/15 = 2\u03c0/5 =72\u00b0, 7\u03c0/15=84\u00b0, which is close to \u03c0/2. Also, note that cos(\u03c0 - \u03b8) = -cos\u03b8. For example, cos7\u03c0/15 = cos84\u00b0, and cos(\u03c0 -84\u00b0)=cos96\u00b0=-cos84\u00b0, but not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, there might be symmetry in the product. Let's list all the terms:\n\ncos(\u03c0/15) \u2248 cos12\u00b0\n\ncos(2\u03c0/15) \u2248 cos24\u00b0\n\ncos(3\u03c0/15)=cos(\u03c0/5)=cos36\u00b0\n\ncos(4\u03c0/15)=cos48\u00b0\n\ncos(5\u03c0/15)=cos(\u03c0/3)=1/2\n\ncos(6\u03c0/15)=cos(2\u03c0/5)=cos72\u00b0\n\ncos(7\u03c0/15)=cos84\u00b0\n\nSo we have cos12\u00b0\u00b7cos24\u00b0\u00b7cos36\u00b0\u00b7cos48\u00b0\u00b7(1/2)\u00b7cos72\u00b0\u00b7cos84\u00b0\n\nFirst, note that 84\u00b0 = 90\u00b0 -6\u00b0, so cos84\u00b0=sin6\u00b0, but not sure. Alternatively, notice that cos84\u00b0 = sin6\u00b0, so maybe pair with something else.