4\n\n3) 3/2\n\n4) 1/128", "deepseek_solution": "(1) To find the value of \\(\\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ}\\):\n\n- Recognize \\(\\sin 30^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), simplifying the product to \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ}\\).\n- Use the identity \\(\\sin \\theta \\cdot \\sin (60^\\circ - \\theta) \\cdot \\sin (60^\\circ + \\theta) = \\frac{\\sin 3\\theta}{4}\\) with \\(\\theta = 10^\\circ\\):\n  \\[\n  \\sin 10^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 50^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 70^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 30^\\circ}{4} = \\frac{1}{8}\n  \\]\n- Multiply by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) to get \\(\\frac{1}{16}\\).\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{16}}\\)\n\n(2) To simplify \\(\\sin^{2} 20^{\\circ} + \\cos^{2} 80^{\\circ} + \\sqrt{3} \\sin 20^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 80^{\\circ}\\):\n\n- Use \\(\\cos 80^\\circ = \\sin 10^\\circ\\) and convert squares to double angles:\n  \\[\n  \\sin^{2} 20^\\circ = \\frac{1 - \\cos 40^\\circ}{2}, \\quad \\cos^{2} 80^\\circ = \\frac{1 + \\cos 160^\\circ}{2}\n  \\]\n- Combine terms and simplify using \\(\\cos 160^\\circ = -\\cos 20^\\circ\\):\n  \\[\n  \\frac{2 - \\cos 40^\\circ - \\cos 20^\\circ}{2} + \\sqrt{3} \\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ\n  \\]\n- Simplify \\(\\sqrt{3} \\sin 20^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ\\) using product-to-sum identities and observe cancellation due to trigonometric identities:\n  \\[\n  \\text{Result simplifies to } \\frac{1}{4}\n  \\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{4}}\\)\n\n(3) To evaluate \\(\\cos^{2} A + \\cos^{2}\\left(60^{\\circ} - A\\right) + \\cos^{2}\\left(60^{\\circ} + A\\right)\\):\n\n- Use \\(\\cos^2 \\theta = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\theta}{2}\\) for each term:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\left[\\cos 2A + \\cos (120^\\circ - 2A) + \\cos (120^\\circ + 2A)\\right]\n  \\]\n- Combine cosines using sum-to-product identities, leading to cancellation:\n  \\[\n  \\text{Result simplifies to } \\frac{3}{2}\n  \\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\)\n\n(4) To compute \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{3 \\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{5 \\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{6 \\pi}{15} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{7 \\pi}{15}\\):\n\n- Recognize the product of cosines of angles \\(\\frac{k\\pi}{15}\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, \\ldots, 7\\).\n- Apply the formula for the product of cosines \\(\\prod_{k=1}^{n} \\cos \\frac{k\\pi}{2n+1} = \\frac{1}{2^n}\\) with \\(n = 7\\):\n  \\[\n  \\text{Result is } \\frac{1}{2^7} = \\frac{1}{128}\n  \\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{128}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "#### Part (1) : Evaluate \\(\\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ}\\)\n\n1. Define \\(A = \\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ} \\) and \\( B = \\cos 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 70^{\\circ} \\).\n2. Consider the product \\( A \\cdot B \\):\n   \\[\n   A \\cdot B = \\left( \\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ} \\right) \\left( \\cos 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 70^{\\circ} \\right)\n   \\]\n3. Using trigonometric identities:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2x\\right)\n   \\]\n   We get:\n   \\[\n   A \\cdot B = \\frac{1}{16} \\sin 20^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 60^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 100^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 140^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{16} \\cos 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\cos 70^{\\circ}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{16} B\n   \\]\n4. Since \\( B \\neq 0 \\), we can divide both sides by \\( B \\):\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{1}{16}\n   \\]\n5. Therefore, we conclude:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 10^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 30^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 50^{\\circ} \\cdot \\sin 70^{\\circ} = \\frac{1}{16}\n   \\]\n\n\