 \\equiv 1 \\mod 625 \\)\n2. \\( N \\equiv 1 \\mod 16 \\) and \\( N \\equiv 0 \\mod 625 \\)\n\nWe can solve these using the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT). Let's tackle each case.\n\n**Case 1: \\( N \\equiv 0 \\mod 16 \\) and \\( N \\equiv 1 \\mod 625 \\)**\n\nWe need to find a number \\( N \\) such that:\n\n\\( N = 16k \\) for some integer \\( k \\), and\n\n\\( 16k \\equiv 1 \\mod 625 \\)\n\nSo, we need to solve \\( 16k \\equiv 1 \\mod 625 \\). To find \\( k \\), we can compute the modular inverse of 16 modulo 625.\n\nLet's compute \\( 16^{-1} \\mod 625 \\). To find the inverse, we can use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFind integers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that \\( 16x + 625y = 1 \\).\n\nLet's apply the algorithm:\n\n625 = 16 * 39 + 1\n\n16 = 1 * 16 + 0\n\nSo, backtracking:\n\n1 = 625 - 16 * 39\n\nTherefore, \\( x = -39 \\) is a solution. To make it positive, add 625 to it: -39 + 625 = 586. So, the inverse of 16 mod 625 is 586.\n\nThus, \\( k \\equiv 586 \\mod 625 \\), so \\( k = 586 + 625m \\) for some integer \\( m \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( N = 16k = 16(586 + 625m) = 16*586 + 16*625m = 9376 + 10000m \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest positive solution in this case is 9376. Adding multiples of 10000 will give other solutions, but since we are looking for numbers ending with the four digits \\( abcd \\), 9376 is the only such number in this case (since 9376 + 10000m will have the same last four digits when m is an integer).\n\n**Case 2: \\( N \\equiv 1 \\mod 16 \\) and \\( N \\equiv 0 \\mod 625 \\)**\n\nHere, \\( N = 625m \\) for some integer \\( m \\), and:\n\n\\( 625m \\equiv 1 \\mod 16 \\)\n\nSince 625 mod 16: 625 \u00f7 16 = 39*16 = 624, so 625 \u2261 1 mod 16.\n\nTherefore, \\( 1 * m \u2261 1 mod 16 \\implies m \u2261 1 mod 16 \\).\n\nTherefore, \\( m = 1 + 16k \\), so:\n\n\\( N = 625(1 + 16k) = 625 + 10000k \\)\n\nThus, the smallest positive solution here is 625. However, 625 is a three-digit number, but when considered as a four-digit number, it would