3 = 0 as derived earlier. Then, plugging in A(0,1,-1) gives 0 -5 -3 +3 = -5 \u2260 0. So, A is not on the image plane.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem was referring to similarity transformation as a more general transformation, including rotation. But if rotation is involved, then the transformation isn't just scaling, so the image plane's orientation could change, making the equation different. But the problem doesn't mention any rotation, just the coefficient k. So, probably, it's just the homothety (scaling and reflection) as we considered.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the question is about whether the point A is mapped to the plane a under the inverse transformation, but the problem states \"belongs to the image of plane a\", so image under the transformation T, not pre-image. So, image of plane a under T is the set { T(P) | P \u2208 a }, and we need to see if A is in that set.\n\nAlternatively, if we think of the transformation as going from the original space to the image space, then the image plane's equation is 6x -5y +3z +3 = 0, as we found. Then checking if A is on that plane. As we saw, plugging in gives -5 \u2260 0. Hence, answer is no.\n\nBut maybe I made a mistake in deriving the image plane equation. Let me check that again.\n\nOriginal plane: 6x -5y +3z -4 = 0.\n\nTransformation is T: (x, y, z) \u21a6 (-3/4 x, -3/4 y, -3/4 z).\n\nSo, for any point (x, y, z) on plane a, the image point is (-3/4 x, -3/4 y, -3/4 z). Let\u2019s denote the image coordinates as (X, Y, Z). Then:\n\nX = -3/4 x \u21d2 x = -4/3 X\n\nY = -3/4 y \u21d2 y = -4/3 Y\n\nZ = -3/4 z \u21d2 z = -4/3 Z\n\nSubstitute these into the original plane equation:\n\n6*(-4/3 X) -5*(-4/3 Y) +3*(-4/3 Z) -4 = 0\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n6*(-4/3 X) = -24/3 X = -8X\n\n-5*(-4/3 Y) = 20/3 Y\n\n3*(-4/3 Z) = -12/3 Z = -4Z\n\nSo, combining:\n\n-8X + (20/3)Y -4Z -4 = 0\n\nMultiply all terms by 3 to eliminate fractions:\n\n-24X + 20Y -12Z -12 = 0\n\nDivide by common factor if possible. Let's see, coeffi