We need \\( n \\) to be the smallest integer such that:\n   \\[\n   7n \\geq 10^{2015}\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   n \\geq \\frac{10^{2015}}{7}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\):**\n   The decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is the repeating decimal 0.142857142857\u2026, which repeats every 6 digits.\n\n5. **Multiplying \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) by \\(10^{2015}\\):**\n   We write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10^{2015}}{7} = 10^{2015} \\times \\frac{1}{7}\n   \\]\n   The effect of multiplying by \\(10^{2015}\\) is moving the decimal point 2015 places to the right.\n\n6. **Locating the decimal point in \\(\\frac{10^{2015}}{7}\\):**\n   The decimal representation of \\(\\frac{10^{2015}}{7}\\) consists of blocks of 142857. Since:\n   \\[\n   2015 = 6 \\times 335 + 5,\n   \\]\n   it means after 335 full repetitions of 142857, we have 14285 before the decimal point. The expansion appears as:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10^{2015}}{7} = 142857142857\\ldots14285714285.7142857\n   \\]\n\n7. **Finding the smallest integer \\(\\left \\lceil \\frac{10^{2015}}{7} \\right \\rceil\\):**\n   Thus, the least integer \\(n\\) such that:\n   \\[\n   n \\geq \\frac{10^{2015}}{7}\n   \\]\n   is:\n   \\[\n   142857142857\\ldots14285714286,\n   \\]\n   ending in the digit 6.\n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, the units digit of the least positive integer \\( n \\) for which \\( 7n \\) has 2016 digits is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{6}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that the number of rotations of a regular polyhedron that map it onto itself is twice the number of its edges. (The identical transformation of space, i.e., rotation by an angle of $0^{\\circ}$, should also be considered a rotation.)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out how to prove that the number of rotations of a regular polyhedron that map it onto itself is twice the number of its edges. Hmm, okay. So, first, I need to recall what a regular polyhedron is. A regular polyhedron, also known as a Platonic solid, is a convex polyhedron with all its faces being regular polygons, and the same number of faces meet at each vertex. There are five Platonic solids: tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron, and icosahedron. Each of these should have symmetries, which include rotations that map the polyhedron onto itself. The problem states that the number of such rotations is twice the number of edges. So, for each Platonic solid, if I can relate their rotational symmetries to twice their edges, that would do it. But how?\n\nLet me start by considering each Platonic solid individually and see if I can compute their rotational symmetry groups and check the relation with edges. Maybe there's a pattern. Let's start with the tetrahedron. A regular tetrahedron has 4 triangular faces, 4 vertices, and 6 edges. The rotational symmetry group of the tetrahedron, which excludes reflections, has 12 elements. If I take twice the number of edges, 6 edges times 2 is 12. Oh, that matches. Similarly, for a cube. A cube has 12 edges. Twice that is 24. The rotational symmetry group of a cube is indeed 24 elements. The octahedron is dual to the cube, so it should have the same symmetry group. The octahedron has 12 edges, so 24 rotational symmetries. That works too. The dodecahedron has 30 edges, so twice that is 60. The rotational symmetry group of the dodecahedron (and its dual, the icosahedron) is 60, which is correct. So, this seems to hold for all five Platonic solids. So, the formula works. But how to prove it in general?\n\nMaybe there's a general approach that works for any regular polyhedron. Let me think about the rotational symmetries. A rotation that maps the polyhedron onto itself must map vertices to vertices, edges to edges, and faces to faces. The key idea might be to count the number of such rotations by considering the effect of the rotations on the polyhedron's elements. Alternatively, perhaps using orbit-stabilizer theorem from group theory.\n\nThe orbit-stabilizer theorem states that the size of a group (in this case, the rotation group) is equal to the number of elements in an orbit multiplied by the size of the stabilizer subgroup for a particular element. So, if I can pick an element (vertex, edge, face) and compute the orbit and stabilizer, maybe I can find the order of the group.\n\nLet me try this. Let's pick an edge. For a regular polyhedron, all edges are in the same orbit under the rotation group. So, the number of edges is the size of the orbit. The stabilizer subgroup of an edge consists of all rotations that map the edge to itself. What rotations do that? Well, since the polyhedron is regular, an edge is between two vertices. A rotation that maps the edge to itself must either swap the two vertices or leave them fixed. However, since we are considering rotational symmetries (orientation-preserving), swapping the two vertices would require a reflection, which is not allowed. So, the only non-trivial rotation that stabilizes the edge is the 180-degree rotation around the axis through the midpoint of the edge and the center of the polyhedron. Let's confirm this.\n\nTake a cube, for example. An edge is between two vertices. The axis through the midpoint of the edge and the center of the cube allows a 180-degree rotation, which swaps the two adjacent faces. This rotation would leave the edge's position invariant but swap the two faces adjacent to it. So, in this case, the stabilizer subgroup for an edge would have order 2: the identity rotation (0 degrees) and the 180-degree rotation. Therefore, by orbit-stabilizer theorem, the order of the rotation group is equal to the number of edges (the orbit size) multiplied by the stabilizer size (2). Hence, the order of the group is 2 * number of edges. That's exactly what we need to prove.\n\nWait, that seems too straightforward. Let me check again. If the orbit is the set of edges, which has size E, and the stabilizer of each edge has size 2 (identity and 180-degree rotation), then the rotation group has size E * 2. So, that's the result. But is the stabilizer really of size 2? Let me check with the tetrahedron. For a tetrahedron, take an edge. What's the stabilizer? The edge connects two vertices. In the tetrahedron's case, a rotation by 180 degrees around the axis through the midpoint of the edge and the center of the tetrahedron swaps the two other vertices. Wait, but in the tetrahedron, each edge is part of two triangles. A 180-degree rotation around the edge's axis swaps the two opposite edges. Wait, maybe in the tetrahedron, the stabilizer is also of order 2? Let's see. The tetrahedron's rotational symmetry group has order 12, and the number of edges is 6. 12 = 6 * 2. So yes, that works. If each edge's stabilizer is size 2, then orbit-stabilizer gives 6 * 2 = 12. Similarly, for the cube, 12 edges, stabilizer size 2, gives 24. So, that seems to hold.\n\nTherefore, the general proof would be: for a regular polyhedron, consider its rotation group. The edges form a single orbit under the rotation group. The stabilizer subgroup of any edge consists of the identity rotation and the 180-degree rotation around the axis through the midpoint of the edge and the center of the polyhedron. Therefore, by orbit-stabilizer theorem, the order of the rotation group is equal to the number of edges multiplied by 2, hence twice the number of edges.\n\nBut let me verify if the orbit of an edge under the rotation group is indeed the entire set of edges. Since the polyhedron is regular, it's vertex-transitive, edge-transitive, and face-transitive. So, any edge can be mapped to any other edge via a rotation. Therefore, the orbit is the entire edge set. Hence, the orbit size is E.\n\nNow, the stabilizer. Let's take an edge e. The stabilizer consists of all rotations that fix e (as a set, not necessarily pointwise). For a regular polyhedron, what rotations fix e? There's the identity rotation. Then, since the polyhedron is regular, there should be a non-trivial rotation that fixes e. The axis of this rotation would pass through the midpoint of e and the center of the polyhedron. Rotating 180 degrees around this axis swaps the two vertices of e and swaps the two faces adjacent to e. Since we're dealing with rotational symmetries (orientation-preserving), this 180-degree rotation is allowed. However, a rotation of 120 degrees around this axis would not map the polyhedron onto itself, because the faces adjacent to e are regular polygons, and their angles would not align after a 120-degree rotation. For example, in a cube, the faces are squares. A 90-degree rotation around the edge's axis would move the adjacent faces, but such a rotation is not a symmetry because it would not preserve the cube's structure. Only the 180-degree rotation works. Therefore, the stabilizer has only two elements: identity and 180-degree rotation. Hence, stabilizer size 2.\n\nTherefore, by orbit-stabilizer theorem, the order of the rotation group is E * 2. Hence, proved.\n\nBut let me check another example to be safe. Take the octahedron. It has 12 edges. The rotational symmetry group should be of order 24 (since octahedron is dual to cube, same symmetry group). 12 edges * 2 = 24. Correct. Similarly, for the dodecahedron: 30 edges * 2 = 60, which is the order of its rotation group. Correct. And the icosahedron, same as dodecahedron. So, this seems consistent.\n\nIs there a case where the stabilizer could be larger? For example, in a regular polygon, but wait, polygons are 2D. But in 3D, for the polyhedron, the key is that the only non-trivial rotation stabilizing an edge is 180 degrees. For example, in a regular tetrahedron, rotating 180 degrees around an edge swaps the other two edges. But is there another rotation that stabilizes the edge? If we rotate around a different axis, but passing through the edge, maybe? Wait, the axis is fixed through the edge's midpoint and the center. In the tetrahedron, an edge's midpoint and center define a line. A 180-degree rotation around that line swaps the two vertices of the edge and also swaps the other two vertices. But is there a rotation of a different angle that would stabilize the edge? Let's see: suppose we try a 120-degree rotation. However, 120 degrees around that axis would not map the tetrahedron onto itself because the tetrahedron doesn't have rotational symmetry of that order around an edge axis. The only symmetries that stabilize the edge are the identity and 180-degree rotation. Hence, stabilizer of size 2. Similarly, in a cube, the 180-degree rotation around the edge axis swaps the two adjacent faces, but a 90-degree rotation would not preserve the cube. Hence, only order 2.\n\nTherefore, the general proof seems solid. The key steps are:\n\n1. Regular polyhedrons are edge-transitive under their rotation groups, so the orbit of any edge is the entire edge set, size E.\n2. The stabilizer subgroup of an edge consists of the identity and a 180-degree rotation around the axis through the edge's midpoint and the polyhedron's center.\n3. By orbit-stabilizer theorem, the rotation group's order is E * 2.\n\nTherefore, the number of rotational symmetries is twice the number of edges.\n\nBut let me just think if there's any hole here. For example, is the stabilizer really only size 2? Suppose there's a polyhedron where more rotations stabilize an edge. But in regular polyhedrons (Platonic solids), due to their high symmetry, the only non-trivial rotation that stabilizes an edge is the 180-degree rotation. For example, in a cube, if you take an edge, rotating it 180 degrees swaps the two adjacent faces. If you tried a 90-degree rotation around that edge's axis, the adjacent faces would rotate, but the cube wouldn't map to itself because the other edges would not align. Similarly, in a tetrahedron, as discussed. So, in all cases, only two elements in the stabilizer.\n\nAnother way to think about it: for each edge, there's a unique rotational axis through its midpoint and center, and the rotational symmetries around that axis are limited to 180 degrees because of the regular polyhedron's structure. Since the faces are regular polygons, their arrangement around the edge only allows for a rotation that aligns them every 180 degrees. Hence, stabilizer of order 2.\n\nTherefore, the proof using orbit-stabilizer seems valid. Hence, the number of rotational symmetries is twice the number of edges.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of rotations is twice the number of edges, so the proof is established as \\boxed{2E}.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the number of rotations of a regular polyhedron that map it onto itself is twice the number of its edges, we can use the orbit-stabilizer theorem from group theory. Here are the key steps:\n\n1. **Orbit-Stabilizer Theorem**: The theorem states that the size of a group (in this case, the rotation group of the polyhedron) is equal to the number of elements in an orbit multiplied by the size of the stabilizer subgroup for a particular element.\n\n2. **Edge-Transitivity**: In a regular polyhedron, all edges are in the same orbit under the rotation group. This means the number of edges (E) is the size of the orbit.\n\n3. **Stabilizer Subgroup**: The stabilizer subgroup of an edge consists of all rotations that map the edge to itself. For a regular polyhedron, the only non-trivial rotation that stabilizes an edge is a 180-degree rotation around the axis through the midpoint of the edge and the center of the polyhedron. This gives the stabilizer subgroup a size of 2 (including the identity rotation).\n\n4. **Applying the Theorem**: Using the orbit-stabilizer theorem, the order of the rotation group (number of rotational symmetries) is the product of the orbit size (E) and the stabilizer size (2). Therefore, the number of rotational symmetries is \\(2 \\times E\\).\n\n### Final Answer\nThe number of rotations is twice the number of edges, so the proof is established as \\boxed{2E}.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Definition of a Rotation of a Polyhedron**:\n   We call a transformation of space around an axis, where a polyhedron maps onto itself, a rotation of the polyhedron.\n\n2. **Relocation of an Edge**:\n   Given that the polyhedron is regular, we can take any fixed edge $[AB]$ and map it to any other edge $[A^{\\prime} B^{\\prime}]$ of the total number $a$ edges of the polyhedron in two rotations:\n   - In one rotation, vertex $A$ maps to $A^{\\prime}$ and vertex $B$ maps to $B^{\\prime}$.\n   - In another rotation, vertex $A$ maps to $B^{\\prime}$ and vertex $B$ maps to $A^{\\prime}$.\n\n3. **Total Rotations**:\n   Therefore, the total number of rotations of the polyhedron equals $2a$, where $a$ is the number of edges.\n\n4. **Generalization**:\n   Similar considerations with faces and vertices yield formulas for the number of rotations $\\mathrm{nf}$ and $mp$, where $m$ and $n$ are the same as in problem 36. For regular polyhedra, $mf=mp=2a$. \n\n**Conclusion**:\nThe number of rotations of space, under which a regular polyhedron maps onto itself, is twice the number of its edges.\n\\[\n\\boxed{2a}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The value of \\(\\frac{10+20+30+40}{10}\\) is:\n(A) 90\n(B) 91\n(C) 10\n(D) 64\n(E) 9", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The question is asking for the value of (10 + 20 + 30 + 40) divided by 10. The options are given from A to E. Hmm, I need to make sure I do this step by step.\n\nFirst, let me tackle the numerator: 10 + 20 + 30 + 40. Let me add those numbers one by one. Starting with 10 and 20. 10 plus 20 is 30. Then adding 30 to that. 30 plus 30 is 60. Next, adding 40. So 60 plus 40 is 100. Wait, so the numerator is 100? Let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. 10 + 20 is 30, plus 30 is 60, plus 40 is 100. Yeah, that seems right. So the sum in the numerator is 100.\n\nNow, the problem says to divide that by 10. So 100 divided by 10. Let me do that division. 100 \u00f7 10 equals 10. So the result should be 10. Let me look at the options again. Option C is 10. So is that the answer? Wait, let me double-check everything because sometimes it's easy to slip up with arithmetic.\n\nStarting over just to be thorough. Adding the numbers in the numerator: 10, 20, 30, 40. Let's add them in pairs. 10 + 40 is 50, and 20 + 30 is also 50. Then 50 + 50 is 100. Yep, that's the same result. So numerator is definitely 100. Dividing by 10 gives 10. So answer C. But wait, the options are (A) 90, (B)91, (C)10, (D)64, (E)9. So C is 10. That seems right.\n\nBut let me check if I read the problem correctly. Sometimes the way the numbers are presented can be tricky. The question is (10 + 20 + 30 + 40) divided by 10. No, that's straightforward. All the numbers in the numerator are being added, then divided by 10. So 100 divided by 10 is 10. Option C. Hmm, but why is option E 9? Maybe someone might miscalculate the numerator as 90, then divide by 10 to get 9? Let me see. If the sum was 90 instead of 100, then 90 divided by 10 is 9. But how would the sum be 90? Let's see: 10 + 20 is 30, 30 + 30 is 60, 60 + 40 is 100. If someone misses one of the numbers, maybe? Like if they added 10 + 20 + 30 + 40 but only added three numbers by mistake. Wait, there are four numbers here: 10, 20, 30, 40. So if someone adds 10 + 20 + 30, that's 60, and then divides by 10 to get 6, which isn't even an option. Or 20 + 30 + 40, which is 90, then divided by 10 is 9, which is option E. So maybe that's why E is there as a trap. But the problem clearly states all four numbers. So the correct sum is 100, divided by 10 is 10. So answer is C. I think that's solid. Let me check once more. 10 plus 20 is 30. 30 plus 30 is 60. 60 plus 40 is 100. 100 divided by 10 is 10. Yes, that's correct. So option C is the right answer.", "deepseek_solution": "The problem requires calculating \\(\\frac{10 + 20 + 30 + 40}{10}\\).\n\n1. **Sum the numerator**:  \n   \\(10 + 20 = 30\\)  \n   \\(30 + 30 = 60\\)  \n   \\(60 + 40 = 100\\).  \n   **Numerator = 100**.\n\n2. **Divide by 10**:  \n   \\(\\frac{100}{10} = 10\\).\n\n**Verification**:  \nAdding in pairs:  \n\\(10 + 40 = 50\\) and \\(20 + 30 = 50\\), then \\(50 + 50 = 100\\).  \nDivision confirms \\(100 \\div 10 = 10\\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\(\\boxed{C}\\) (10)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's solve the given expression step-by-step:\n\n1. **Sum the Numerator**: \n\n   The numerator of the fraction is \\(10 + 20 + 30 + 40\\). We need to add these values together:\n   \\[\n   10 + 20 + 30 + 40 \n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the Addition**:\n\n   Add the numbers sequentially:\n   \\[\n   10 + 20 = 30\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30 + 30 = 60\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   60 + 40 = 100\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   10 + 20 + 30 + 40 = 100\n   \\]\n\n3. **Divide by 10**:\n\n   Now we divide the sum obtained by 10:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100}{10}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the Division**:\n\n   Divide:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{100}{10} = 10\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion**: \n\nThe value of \\(\\frac{10+20+30+40}{10}\\) is \\(10\\), which corresponds to option (C).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{C}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $x_{1}, \\ldots, x_{n}$ be the roots of the polynomial\n\n$$\nx^{n}+a_{n-1} x^{n-1}+a_{n-2} x^{n-2}+\\ldots+a_{0}\n$$\n\nProve that $x_{1}+x_{2}+\\ldots+x_{n}=-a_{n-1}$,\n\n$$\n\\sum_{1 \\leqslant i<j \\leqslant n} x_{i} x_{j}=a_{n-2}, \\quad \\sum_{1 \\leqslant i<j<k \\leqslant n} x_{i} x_{j} x_{k}=-a_{n-3}, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nx_{1} \\ldots x_{n}=(-1)^{n} a_{0}\n$$\n\n(known as Vieta's formulas).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove Vieta's formulas for a general polynomial. Vieta's formulas relate the coefficients of a polynomial to sums and products of its roots. The problem statement gives the specific relationships, like the sum of roots being -a_{n-1}, the sum of products of roots two at a time being a_{n-2}, and so on, with the product of all roots being (-1)^n a_0. \n\nFirst, I should recall Vieta's formulas for lower-degree polynomials and see if I can generalize the pattern. For example, for a quadratic equation x\u00b2 + bx + c = 0, the roots satisfy x\u2081 + x\u2082 = -b and x\u2081x\u2082 = c. Similarly, for a cubic x\u00b3 + ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0, the sum of roots is -a, the sum of products two at a time is b, and the product of roots is -c. So the pattern seems to be alternating signs depending on the degree and the number of roots multiplied together.\n\nGiven that the polynomial is monic (leading coefficient 1), the formulas have coefficients with signs alternating based on the number of terms in the product. The problem states that the sum of roots is -a_{n-1}, which aligns with the quadratic and cubic examples. The product of all roots is (-1)^n a_0. For the quadratic, n=2, so (-1)^2 a_0 = a_0, but in the quadratic case, the product is c, which is indeed a_0 here. For the cubic, product is -c, which is (-1)^3 times the constant term, which matches. So the sign alternates based on the degree.\n\nTo approach the proof, maybe expanding the polynomial in terms of its roots and comparing coefficients will work. If the polynomial can be written as (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082)...(x - x\u2099), then expanding this product should give the monic polynomial with coefficients expressed in terms of the roots. The coefficients can then be matched to the given polynomial's coefficients, leading to Vieta's formulas.\n\nLet me try writing the polynomial as the product of linear factors. If x\u2081, x\u2082, ..., x\u2099 are the roots, then the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\nP(x) = (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082)...(x - x\u2099)\n\nExpanding this product will give a polynomial of the form x\u207f + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + ... + a\u2080. The coefficients a_{n-1}, a_{n-2}, ..., a\u2080 can be found by expanding the product. Vieta's formulas state that these coefficients are related to the roots in specific ways.\n\nLet me recall how expansion works. When expanding (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082)...(x - x\u2099), each term in the expansion is a product of either x or -x_i from each factor. The coefficient of x^{n-1} comes from choosing x from all but one of the factors and -x_i from one factor. So the coefficient of x^{n-1} is the sum of all -x_i, which is - (x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099). Therefore, a_{n-1} = - (x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099), which rearranged gives the sum of roots as -a_{n-1}.\n\nSimilarly, the coefficient a_{n-2} is the sum of all products of roots taken two at a time, each multiplied by (-1)^2. Wait, but when expanding, the coefficient of x^{n-2} is obtained by choosing x from (n-2) factors and -x_i from two factors. The product of those two -x_i terms is (-1)^2 x_i x_j = x_i x_j. Since the combinations are taken over all pairs, the coefficient a_{n-2} is equal to the sum over all i < j of x_i x_j. So that's exactly the second formula: \u03a3 x_i x_j = a_{n-2}.\n\nContinuing this logic, the coefficient of x^{n-k} is the sum over all products of k roots, each multiplied by (-1)^k. Therefore, a_{n-k} = (-1)^k \u03a3 x_{i\u2081}x_{i\u2082}...x_{i_k}}, where the sum is over all combinations of k roots. So for example, when k=3, a_{n-3} = (-1)^3 \u03a3 x_i x_j x_k, so \u03a3 x_i x_j x_k = -a_{n-3}. This matches the given formula. \n\nFor the constant term a\u2080, which is the coefficient of x\u2070, we need to take the product of all -x_i terms. So a\u2080 = (-1)^n x\u2081x\u2082...x\u2099. Therefore, solving for the product of roots gives x\u2081x\u2082...x\u2099 = (-1)^n a\u2080, which is the last formula.\n\nTherefore, by expanding the product (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082)...(x - x\u2099) and equating the coefficients with the given polynomial, we derive Vieta's formulas. Each coefficient a_{n-k} corresponds to (-1)^k times the sum of products of the roots taken k at a time.\n\nWait, let me check this again for the coefficient of x^{n-k}. When expanding the product, to get the term x^{n-k}, we need to choose k factors from which we take the -x_i term and x from the remaining (n - k) factors. The product of the k terms (-x_i) is (-1)^k times the product of the roots. Since each combination of k roots is considered, the coefficient of x^{n - k} is the sum over all combinations of k roots multiplied by (-1)^k. Therefore, the coefficient a_{n - k} is equal to (-1)^k times the sum of the products of the roots taken k at a time. \n\nTherefore, Vieta's formula states that the sum of products of roots taken k at a time is equal to (-1)^k a_{n - k}. However, in the problem statement, the formulas are presented with alternating signs. Let me verify with the given examples:\n\n- For k = 1: Sum of roots x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099 = -a_{n-1}. According to our formula, this should be (-1)^1 a_{n - 1} = -a_{n-1}, which matches.\n\n- For k = 2: Sum of products two at a time is a_{n-2}. According to our formula, it's (-1)^2 a_{n - 2} = a_{n-2}, which matches.\n\n- For k = 3: Sum of products three at a time is -a_{n-3}, which according to our formula is (-1)^3 a_{n - 3} = -a_{n - 3}, matching again.\n\nThus, in general, the sum of products of roots taken k at a time is equal to (-1)^k a_{n - k}. So the problem statement is accurate. \n\nTherefore, the proof would involve expanding the factored form of the polynomial and showing that each coefficient a_{n - k} is indeed equal to (-1)^k times the sum of products of roots taken k at a time. Since the polynomial is monic, the leading term is x\u207f, and the other terms are built from the roots as described.\n\nI should formalize this expansion process. Let's consider the polynomial P(x) = (x - x\u2081)(x - x\u2082)...(x - x\u2099). Expanding this product gives a sum over all subsets of the factors, choosing either x or -x_i from each factor. The coefficient of x^{n - k} is the sum over all possible ways to choose k roots (from the n factors) to take the -x_i term, and x from the remaining n - k factors. Each such term contributes (-1)^k times the product of the k chosen roots. Therefore, the coefficient a_{n - k} is equal to (-1)^k times the sum of all possible products of k roots. This is exactly Vieta's formula.\n\nTo make this rigorous, perhaps using induction on the degree n. For the base case, n = 1: P(x) = x + a\u2080. The root is x\u2081 = -a\u2080. Vieta's formula would say x\u2081 = -a\u2080, which is correct since for n=1, x\u2081 = (-1)^1 a\u2080, which is -a\u2080. \n\nAssume the formula holds for polynomials of degree n - 1. Then for a polynomial of degree n, write it as (x - x\u2099) times a polynomial of degree n - 1 with roots x\u2081, ..., x_{n-1}. The coefficients of the degree n polynomial can be related to those of the degree n - 1 polynomial. By expanding (x - x\u2099)(x^{n-1} + b_{n-2}x^{n-2} + ... + b\u2080), we get x\u207f + (b_{n-2} - x\u2099)x^{n-1} + ... + (-x\u2099)b\u2080. Comparing coefficients, each coefficient a_{k} of the original polynomial is b_{k - 1} - x\u2099 b_{k} for the appropriate indices. Then, by induction, we can express the sums of products of roots in terms of these coefficients. However, this approach might get complicated with indices.\n\nAlternatively, using the general expansion of the product (x - x\u2081)...(x - x\u2099). The expansion can be expressed using elementary symmetric sums. The elementary symmetric sum of degree k, denoted S_k, is the sum of all products of k roots. Vieta's formulas state that S_k = (-1)^k a_{n - k}.\n\nThe connection between the coefficients of the polynomial and the elementary symmetric sums is exactly what Vieta's formulas describe. Therefore, if we can show that when expanding the product, the coefficient a_{n - k} is indeed (-1)^k S_k, then the proof is complete.\n\nTo formalize, consider expanding the product:\n\nP(x) = \u220f_{i=1}^n (x - x_i) = \u2211_{k=0}^n (-1)^k S_k x^{n - k}\n\nHere, S_0 = 1 (by convention), S_1 = x\u2081 + x\u2082 + ... + x\u2099, S_2 = \u2211_{i < j} x_i x_j, ..., S_n = x\u2081x\u2082...x\u2099.\n\nComparing this to the given polynomial:\n\nP(x) = x\u207f + a_{n - 1}x^{n - 1} + ... + a\u2080\n\nWe can equate coefficients:\n\nFor each k from 0 to n, the coefficient of x^{n - k} is (-1)^k S_k. Therefore:\n\na_{n - k} = (-1)^k S_k\n\nRearranging gives S_k = (-1)^k a_{n - k}\n\nWhich is Vieta's formula. For example:\n\n- When k = 1: S_1 = (-1)^1 a_{n - 1} => x\u2081 + ... + x\u2099 = -a_{n - 1}\n\n- When k = 2: S_2 = (-1)^2 a_{n - 2} => \u2211_{i < j} x_i x_j = a_{n - 2}\n\n- ...\n\n- When k = n: S_n = (-1)^n a_0 => x\u2081x\u2082...x\u2099 = (-1)^n a_0\n\nThus, Vieta's formulas are established by expanding the polynomial in terms of its roots and matching coefficients with the given polynomial. Each coefficient corresponds to an elementary symmetric sum multiplied by a sign factor based on the number of roots involved in the product.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete by expanding the product of linear factors and comparing coefficients, which directly yields the relationships stated in Vieta's formulas.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove Vieta's formulas, consider a monic polynomial of degree \\( n \\) with roots \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\):\n\n\\[\nP(x) = (x - x_1)(x - x_2) \\cdots (x - x_n) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + a_{n-2}x^{n-2} + \\ldots + a_0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding the product form, the coefficients \\( a_{n-k} \\) are determined by the elementary symmetric sums of the roots:\n\n1. **Expansion and Coefficients**:\n   - The coefficient of \\( x^{n-1} \\) arises from choosing \\( x \\) from \\( n-1 \\) factors and \\( -x_i \\) from one factor. Summing over all roots:\n     \\[\n     a_{n-1} = - (x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n) \\implies x_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n = -a_{n-1}.\n     \\]\n   - The coefficient of \\( x^{n-2} \\) comes from choosing \\( x \\) from \\( n-2 \\) factors and \\( -x_i, -x_j \\) from two distinct factors. Summing all pairwise products:\n     \\[\n     a_{n-2} = \\sum_{1 \\le i < j \\le n} x_i x_j \\implies \\sum_{1 \\le i < j \\le n} x_i x_j = a_{n-2}.\n     \\]\n   - Similarly, the coefficient of \\( x^{n-k} \\) involves products of \\( k \\) roots. For each \\( k \\), the coefficient is:\n     \\[\n     a_{n-k} = (-1)^k \\sum_{1 \\le i_1 < i_2 < \\ldots < i_k \\le n} x_{i_1} x_{i_2} \\cdots x_{i_k}.\n     \\]\n     Rearranging gives:\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{1 \\le i_1 < \\ldots < i_k \\le n} x_{i_1} \\cdots x_{i_k} = (-1)^k a_{n-k}.\n     \\]\n   - The constant term \\( a_0 \\) is the product of all \\( -x_i \\):\n     \\[\n     a_0 = (-1)^n x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n \\implies x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n = (-1)^n a_0.\n     \\]\n\n2. **General Form**:\n   For each \\( k = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\), the sum of products of roots taken \\( k \\) at a time (elementary symmetric sum \\( S_k \\)) satisfies:\n   \\[\n   S_k = (-1)^k a_{n - k}.\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion**:\nBy expanding the polynomial in its factored form and equating coefficients, Vieta's formulas are established, linking the coefficients of the polynomial to the sums and products of its roots:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_1 + x_2 + \\ldots + x_n &= -a_{n-1}, \\\\\n\\sum_{1 \\le i < j \\le n} x_i x_j &= a_{n-2}, \\\\\n\\sum_{1 \\le i < j < k \\le n} x_i x_j x_k &= -a_{n-3}, \\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\nx_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n &= (-1)^n a_0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Consider the given polynomial:\n\\[ P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + a_{n-2} x^{n-2} + \\ldots + a_0 \\]\n\n2. According to Vieta's formulas, since \\(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n\\) are the roots of the polynomial \\(P(x)\\), we can express the polynomial as:\n\\[ P(x) = (x - x_1)(x - x_2) \\cdots (x - x_n) \\]\n\n3. Expanding this product, we obtain:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - x_1)(x - x_2) \\cdots (x - x_n) & = x^n - \\left(\\sum_{1 \\leq i \\leq n} x_i\\right) x^{n-1} \\\\\n& \\quad + \\left(\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} x_i x_j\\right) x^{n-2} \\\\\n& \\quad - \\left(\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} x_i x_j x_k\\right) x^{n-3} \\\\\n& \\quad + \\ldots \\\\\n& \\quad + (-1)^n x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n4. By comparing the coefficients on both sides of the polynomial expansion, we derive the following relationships:\n   - The coefficient of \\(x^{n-1}\\) is:\n     \\[\n     -a_{n-1} = - \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i\n     \\]\n     Therefore,\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = -a_{n-1}\n     \\]\n\n   - The coefficient of \\(x^{n-2}\\) is:\n     \\[\n     a_{n-2} = \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} x_i x_j\n     \\]\n\n   - The coefficient of \\(x^{n-3}\\) is:\n     \\[\n     -a_{n-3} = - \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} x_i x_j x_k\n     \\]\n     Therefore,\n     \\[\n     \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} x_i x_j x_k = a_{n-3}\n     \\]\n\n   - The constant term is:\n     \\[\n     (-1)^n a_0 = (-1)^n x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n\n     \\]\n     Therefore,\n     \\[\n     x_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n = (-1)^n a_0\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion\nThe expressions of the sums and products of the roots are given by:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i &= -a_{n-1}, \\\\\n\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} x_i x_j &= a_{n-2}, \\\\\n\\sum_{1 \\leq i < j < k \\leq n} x_i x_j x_k &= -a_{n-3}, \\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\nx_1 x_2 \\cdots x_n &= (-1)^n a_0 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Mr. Ganzgenau wants to take his tea cup out of the microwave exactly at the front. However, his microwave is not that precise. \n\nThe following game is played:\n\nLet \\( n \\) be a positive integer. In \\( n \\) seconds, the microwave's turntable completes a full rotation. Each time the microwave is turned on, it rotates a whole number of seconds either clockwise or counterclockwise, resulting in \\( n \\) possible positions where the tea cup can come to rest, one of which is ex