s less than R\u00b3/6 \u2248 0.166R\u00b3. Wait, this contradicts. Wait, something is wrong here.\n\nWait, no, if we fix one vertex at the origin, and the other three vertices are vectors b, c, d with |b| = |c| = |d| = R, then the volume is 1/6 |det(b, c, d)|. The Hadamard inequality says that |det(b, c, d)| \u2264 |b||c||d| = R\u00b3, so V \u2264 R\u00b3/6. But the regular tetrahedron's volume is 8R\u00b3/(9\u221a3) \u2248 0.513R\u00b3/6 \u2248 0.0855R\u00b3, which is much less than R\u00b3/6. Wait, no. Wait, there's confusion here. If all vectors are from the origin to the vertices, then the regular tetrahedron's volume is not given by 8R\u00b3/(9\u221a3). That formula is when the tetrahedron is regular with all edges equal and circumradius R. But if we fix one vertex at the origin and the others at vectors of length R, the regular tetrahedron in that setup would have different edge lengths.\n\nThis indicates that fixing one vertex at the origin and others on the sphere of radius R does not give the same configuration as a regular tetrahedron inscribed in a sphere with all vertices at distance R from the center.\n\nTherefore, this approach is not directly applicable.\n\nGiven the time I've spent trying different methods and recalling that the regular tetrahedron is the one with maximum volume inscribed in a sphere, I think the most straightforward way is to cite that this is a known result and provide the example of the regular tetrahedron's volume and circumradius relationship as the maximum case, and then argue that deviations from regularity would decrease the volume.\n\nBut since the problem requires a proof, I need to be more rigorous. Here's a plan:\n\n1. Show that for any tetrahedron inscribed in a sphere of radius R, the volume V is maximized when the tetrahedron is regular.\n\n2. Compute the volume and circumradius relation for the regular tetrahedron, obtaining V = 8/(9\u221a3) R\u00b3.\n\n3. Conclude that for any tetrahedron, V \u2264 8/(9\u221a3) R\u00