nly two points. Similarly, starting from (2,1): (2,1), (3,3), (4,5). Then next would be (5,7), which is outside. So, three points: (2,1), (3,3), (4,5). Similarly, starting from (3,1): (3,1), (4,3), (5,5). That's three points. Similarly, starting from (1,3): (1,3), (2,5), next is (3,7) which is outside. So only two points. Similarly, in the other direction.\n\nSo, for slope 2, there are lines with exactly three points. How many such lines?\n\nLooking at the above examples:\n\n- (1,1), (2,3), (3,5)\n\n- (2,1), (3,3), (4,5)\n\n- (3,1), (4,3), (5,5)\n\nSimilarly, going in the negative direction (slope -2):\n\nStarting from (1,5), next (2,3), (3,1). So, (1,5), (2,3), (3,1). That's three points.\n\nSimilarly, (1,4), (2,2), (3,0) which is invalid. So, starting from (2,5): (2,5), (3,3), (4,1). Three points.\n\n(3,5), (4,3), (5,1). Three points.\n\nSo, slope -2 has similar lines:\n\n- (1,5), (2,3), (3,1)\n\n- (2,5), (3,3), (4,1)\n\n- (3,5), (4,3), (5,1)\n\nSo, for slope 2 and -2, each direction has 3 lines with three points. So, total of 3 + 3 = 6 lines. Each line has exactly 3 points, so each contributes C(3,3)=1 triplet. So, total collinear triplets for slope 2 and -2: 6 lines * 1 triplet each = 6.\n\nSimilarly, slopes 1/2: this is a gentler slope. Let's check if there are lines with slope 1/2 that have three points.\n\nSlope 1/2 means for every 2 units in x, y increases by 1. So, starting from (1,1), next point would be (3,2), then (5,3). So, points (1,1), (3,2), (5,3). Are there three points here. Let's check if these are in the grid. x=1,3,5 and y=1,2,3. Yes, all points are valid. So, this is a line with three points.\n\nSimilarly, starting from (1,2): (1,2), (3,3), (5,4). Three points.\n\n(1,3): (1,3), (3,4), (5,5). Three points.\n\nSimilarly, starting from (2,1): (2,1), (4,2), (6,3). But x=6 is outside, so only two points.\n\n(2,2): (2,2), (4,3), (6,4). Again, x=6 invalid.\n\nSimilarly, starting from (3,1): (3,1), (5,2). Only two points.\n\nNow, in the other direction